                <pb facs="0211" n="105" sameAs="frus:frus1906p1/pg105"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wilson to Mr. de Cuvelier, secretary-general of
                        the Independent State of the Kongo, May 19, 1906.</hi></head>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Secretary-General</hi>: I am in receipt of a
                    dispatch from the Department of State at Washington, inclosing a copy of a
                    letter from Mr. McLean, president of the Foreign Christian Missionary Society,
                    of Cincinnati, in which the complaint is made that the officials of the Kongo
                    Free State decline to sell land to the society for the location of schools,
                    chapels, homes, and hospitals. The station of this society is at Bolengi, but no
                    information is furnished as to the location of the land desired.</p>
                <p>I will thank you, Mr. Secretary-General, for information as to the present
                    attitude of the Kongo Free State relative to applications of this character. I
                    inclose herewith a copy of the letter of Mr. McLean.</p>
                <p>Please accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry Lane
                    Wilson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
